---

<Layout
  title="Devices & Hardware | NTX Mesh"
  description="Device options and recommendations for joining the NTX Mesh Meshtastic network, including pocket nodes and solar-powered repeater build guides."
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="page-hero border-b border-mesh-border bg-mesh-surface">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6">
      <h1>Devices & Hardware</h1>
      <p class="mt-4">
        NTX Mesh runs on <a href="https://meshtastic.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-mesh-green hover:underline">Meshtastic</a>-compatible radios called <strong class="text-mesh-text">nodes</strong>. Here's everything you need to choose one, buy one, or build your own solar repeater.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Page nav -->
  <nav class="sticky top-16 z-40 bg-mesh-bg/90 backdrop-blur-md border-b border-mesh-border">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6">
      <ul class="flex overflow-x-auto gap-1 py-2 text-sm no-scrollbar">
        <li><a href="#options" class="inline-block px-3 py-1.5 text-mesh-muted hover:text-mesh-green rounded-md whitespace-nowrap transition-colors">Device Options</a></li>
        <li><a href="#pocket-portable" class="inline-block px-3 py-1.5 text-mesh-muted hover:text-mesh-green rounded-md whitespace-nowrap transition-colors">Pocket / Portable</a></li>
        <li><a href="#stationary-solar" class="inline-block px-3 py-1.5 text-mesh-muted hover:text-mesh-green rounded-md whitespace-nowrap transition-colors">Stationary / Solar</a></li>
        <li><a href="#solar" class="inline-block px-3 py-1.5 text-mesh-muted hover:text-mesh-green rounded-md whitespace-nowrap transition-colors">Build a Repeater</a></li>
        <li><a href="#history" class="inline-block px-3 py-1.5 text-mesh-muted hover:text-mesh-green rounded-md whitespace-nowrap transition-colors">History & Lessons</a></li>
        <li><a href="#parts-list" class="inline-block px-3 py-1.5 text-mesh-muted hover:text-mesh-green rounded-md whitespace-nowrap transition-colors">Parts List</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-16">

    <!-- Options intro -->
    <section id="options">
      <h2 class="section-heading" id="pocket-portable">Pocket &amp; Portable Nodes</h2>
      <p class="text-mesh-muted mb-8 max-w-3xl">
        These are handheld nodes you carry with you. They connect to the Meshtastic app on your phone via Bluetooth and act as both a personal radio and a mesh relay.
      </p>

      <!-- Complexity legend -->
      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 mb-6 text-sm">
        <span class="flex items-center gap-2 text-mesh-muted">Complexity:</span>
        <span class="badge-easy">1 — Buy it assembled</span>
        <span class="badge-medium">3 — Some assembly</span>
        <span class="badge-advanced">5 — Full DIY build</span>
      </div>

      <!-- Device table (desktop) -->
      <div class="hidden md:block overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-mesh-border">
        <table class="table-mesh w-full">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Family</th>
              <th>Model / Variant</th>
              <th>Complexity</th>
              <th>Type</th>
              <th>Est. Cost</th>
              <th>Links</th>
              <th>Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {portableDevices.map(d => (
              <tr>
                <td class="font-medium text-mesh-text">{d.family}</td>
                <td class="font-mono text-sm">{d.name}</td>
                <td>
                  <span class={d.badge === 'easy' ? 'badge-easy' : d.badge === 'medium' ? 'badge-medium' : 'badge-advanced'}>
                    {d.type === 'buy' && d.buyComplexity ? `Buy: ${d.buyComplexity}` : ''}
                    {d.type === 'build' && d.buildComplexity ? `Build: ${d.buildComplexity}` : ''}
                    {d.type === 'either' ? `Buy: ${d.buyComplexity ?? '?'} / Build: ${d.buildComplexity ?? '?'}` : ''}
                  </span>
                </td>
                <td class="capitalize text-mesh-muted text-sm">{d.type}</td>
                <td class="font-mono text-mesh-green font-medium">{d.priceRange}</td>
                <td class="text-sm">
                  <div class="flex flex-col gap-1">
                    {d.links.map(l => (
                      <a href={l.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-mesh-green hover:underline whitespace-nowrap">{l.label} ↗</a>
                    ))}
                  </div>
                </td>
                <td class="text-mesh-muted text-sm max-w-[200px]">{d.notes ?? '—'}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <!-- Device cards (mobile) -->
      <div class="md:hidden space-y-4">
        {portableDevices.map(d => (
          <div class="card">
            <div class="flex items-start justify-between mb-2">
              <div>
                <div class="text-mesh-muted text-xs font-mono mb-0.5">{d.family}</div>
                <h3 class="font-mono font-bold text-mesh-text">{d.name}</h3>
              </div>
              <span class="font-mono text-mesh-green font-bold text-sm">{d.priceRange}</span>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
              <span class={d.badge === 'easy' ? 'badge-easy' : d.badge === 'medium' ? 'badge-medium' : 'badge-advanced'}>
                {d.type === 'buy' && 'Buy assembled'}
                {d.type === 'build' && 'DIY build'}
                {d.type === 'either' && 'Buy or build'}
              </span>
            </div>
            {d.notes && <p class="text-mesh-muted text-sm mb-3">{d.notes}</p>}
            <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
              {d.links.map(l => (
                <a href={l.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-xs text-mesh-green border border-mesh-green/30 px-2 py-1 rounded-md hover:bg-mesh-green/10 transition-colors">
                  {l.label} ↗
                </a>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Stationary/Solar section -->
    <section id="stationary-solar" class="mt-16 pt-8 border-t border-mesh-border">
      <h2 class="section-heading">Stationary &amp; Solar Nodes</h2>
      <p class="text-mesh-muted mb-8 max-w-3xl">
        Fixed solar nodes dramatically extend the network. Mount them high — on a roof, pole, or tree — and they relay messages 24/7 without any power connection. See the full build guide below.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
        <div class="card border-mesh-green/30">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
            <span class="badge-medium">Intermediate</span>
            <span class="font-mono text-mesh-green text-sm">~$185</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="font-mono font-bold text-mesh-text mb-2">V5 Solar Repeater</h3>
          <p class="text-mesh-muted text-sm mb-4">
            Our current recommended build. 12W solar panel, Voltaic V25 battery, RAK board, fiberglass antenna. No soldering required. Has been running reliably in Texas heat.
          </p>
          <a href="#parts-list" class="text-mesh-green text-sm hover:underline">See parts list ↓</a>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
            <span class="badge-easy">Buy</span>
            <span class="font-mono text-mesh-green text-sm">$100–$200</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="font-mono font-bold text-mesh-text mb-2">Pre-built Solar Node</h3>
          <p class="text-mesh-muted text-sm mb-4">
            Buy a fully assembled solar repeater from Etsy. Ready to mount. Good option if you want coverage fast without building.
          </p>
          <a href="https://www.etsy.com/search?q=meshtastic+solar+repeater" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-mesh-green text-sm hover:underline">Search Etsy ↗</a>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
            <span class="badge-easy">Easiest</span>
            <span class="font-mono text-mesh-green text-sm">~$185 + birdhouse</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="font-mono font-bold text-mesh-text mb-2">Solar Birdhouse</h3>
          <p class="text-mesh-muted text-sm mb-4">
            The V5 build disguised as a birdhouse — great for neighborhoods with HOA restrictions. Paint the top black, glue on the solar panel, and you're good to go.
          </p>
          <a href="#parts-list" class="text-mesh-green text-sm hover:underline">See birdhouse parts ↓</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Solar repeater guide -->
    <section id="solar" class="mt-16 pt-8 border-t border-mesh-border prose-mesh">
      <h2>Building a Solar-Powered Repeater</h2>
      <p>
        A solar repeater is the single best thing you can do for the North Texas mesh network. Here's everything we've learned after years of trial and error.
      </p>

      <!-- History -->
      <h3 id="history">A Brief History of Our Builds</h3>
      <p>We have tried 5 permutations of solar powering nodes:</p>

      <div class="not-prose space-y-3 my-6">
        <details class="bg-mesh-surface border border-mesh-border rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
          <summary class="px-4 py-3 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-mesh-muted hover:text-mesh-text transition-colors flex items-center justify-between">
            <span>Version 1.0 — RAKBox-B2 with 2000mAh LiPo</span>
            <span class="text-red-400 text-sm font-normal ml-2">Failed in 1 month</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="px-4 pb-4 text-mesh-muted text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Using the RAKBox-B2 Enclosure with solar panel connected directly to the RAK Wireless WisBlock starter kit. We drilled a hole and attached a fiberglass antenna with an iPEX to N-Type connector. Mounted on a UT building. The 0.4W panel was far too small.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-mesh-surface border border-mesh-border rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
          <summary class="px-4 py-3 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-mesh-muted hover:text-mesh-text transition-colors flex items-center justify-between">
            <span>Version 2.0 — USB Solar (5W)</span>
            <span class="text-red-400 text-sm font-normal ml-2">Failed in 1 month</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="px-4 pb-4 text-mesh-muted text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Same as 1.0 but added a second 5W solar panel via USB. Also failed within a month. Still not enough power.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-mesh-surface border border-mesh-border rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
          <summary class="px-4 py-3 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-mesh-muted hover:text-mesh-text transition-colors flex items-center justify-between">
            <span>Version 3.0 — Internal Solar with 18650s</span>
            <span class="text-red-400 text-sm font-normal ml-2">Failed in 3 months</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="px-4 pb-4 text-mesh-muted text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Larger clear-lid box with solar panel inside, TI Green Power Module, four 18650 batteries, and overdischarge protection. Mounted on UT buildings. Lasted three months before dying.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-mesh-surface border border-mesh-border rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
          <summary class="px-4 py-3 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-mesh-muted hover:text-mesh-text transition-colors flex items-center justify-between">
            <span>Version 4.0 — External Solar Panel</span>
            <span class="text-red-400 text-sm font-normal ml-2">Failed in 3 months</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="px-4 pb-4 text-mesh-muted text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Same as 3.0 with solar panel mounted outside the box. Still died within three months.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details open class="bg-mesh-surface border border-mesh-green/30 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
          <summary class="px-4 py-3 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-mesh-text flex items-center justify-between">
            <span>Version 5.0 — Current Design (Recommended)</span>
            <span class="text-mesh-green text-sm font-normal ml-2">Running reliably</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="px-4 pb-4 text-mesh-muted text-sm leading-relaxed">
            We abandoned the RAK's built-in solar and battery connectors entirely. Instead: a 12W solar panel connects to a Voltaic Systems V25 battery via USB, and the battery connects to the RAK board via USB. The Voltaic V25 acts as the solar charge controller and overdischarge protection. It charges at a wide voltage range, shuts off when the battery is critically low, and automatically restarts when solar has recharged it. The RAK radio uses 100–1000 mAh/day (avg ~400), so the 6,400 mAh V25 theoretically runs it for 16 days without sun.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <h3>Lessons Learned</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Don't trust the RAK board for solar power management.</strong> The Nordic nRF52840 inside the RAK 4631 enters a "Super Deep Sleep" fault when supplied with 3.3V — which happens when the battery voltage drops too low. It requires a manual physical reset to recover. Use the Voltaic V25 to handle all power management instead.</li>
        <li><strong>Solar panels: bigger is better.</strong> 10W or more is ideal. Anything smaller struggles in partial shade or winter.</li>
        <li><strong>Battery: bigger is better.</strong> 5,000 mAh or more is ideal. The Voltaic V25 (6,400 mAh) is our recommendation.</li>
        <li><strong>Use fiberglass antennas.</strong> Rubber duck antennas degrade quickly in UV, wind, and rain. Fiberglass lasts years outdoors.</li>
        <li><strong>Shade the battery.</strong> Texas heat can kill LiPo batteries. Mount the box in shade whenever possible.</li>
        <li><strong>Disable GPS on repeater nodes.</strong> The GPS module dramatically increases power consumption. Stationary repeaters don't need it.</li>
        <li><strong>Seal everything and add a drain plug.</strong> Use O-rings on the antenna mount, wrap connectors in Proxicast, and add a drain plug so condensation can escape. In high-humidity environments, apply conformal coating to the electronics.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        These lessons come from Austin, Texas where we had 80 days over 100°F in 2023 — including 11 days over 105°F. The design should also work well for other hot, humid climates. See <a href="https://weatherspark.com/h/y/8004/2023/Historical-Weather-during-2023-in-Austin-Texas-United-States" target="_blank" rel="noopener">weather history here</a>.
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>Details on boards that failed</summary>
        One was filled with water — the antenna seal failed, which is why the vent valve at the bottom is so important. One died because it got stuck in the low-voltage fault state, now fixed with the Voltaic packs. The third died from unknown causes (likely heat) — when we tried to recover it with wall power, it wouldn't turn on. That one had also been sprayed with a conformal coating we no longer recommend.
      </details>

      <h4>Considerations for Other Climates</h4>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Northern Climates:</strong> Less winter sunshine means you need a larger panel and a lower tilt angle (rule of thumb: angle = your latitude in degrees). Bigger batteries also help since cold temperatures reduce capacity.</li>
        <li><strong>Coastal Climates:</strong> Salt spray and fog condensation kill electronics quickly. Use conformal coating and check seals regularly.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- Parts list -->
    <section id="parts-list" class="mt-16 pt-8 border-t border-mesh-border">
      <h2 class="section-heading">Parts List — V5 Solar Repeater</h2>
      <p class="text-mesh-muted mb-2 max-w-3xl">
        This design requires no soldering, no complicated connectors, and no battery management chips. The solar panel connects directly to the Voltaic V25 via USB, and the Voltaic connects to the RAK board via USB.
      </p>
